#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "d6c73b7b-4981-5908-b694-be7f0e57b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-5490" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below. \nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3,$ then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$\n\n![](images/5f/5f1bfa33d135d95e3153e3305da50b69541d88d1cd78b5b6ad030a89861f3e10.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=x_3.$ From the first equation, we get $x_1=3x_3.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/f2/f2707b01c13749362887e4ae0da130466ae753dccd4bc354600af79f71c3de5f.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7627a6a515086ff53f5ed250a070e08ee2b71d0e4fb3d79e5434218d028624.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{c}=\\dfrac{1}{1}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "b087e601-c054-563c-b7ee-43e18c74ac51" :question/math-academy-id "e-5491" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n![](images/33/33471f345fdb027b0440014631bf0dadff706ffb76938b67b15db66fd80dcae6.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get $x_1=-x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/97/97b9d021ad0a9800e0b6a2b696c3908f2f5bec64fa496a1c2222ae5b86a7b56b.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n![](images/b4/b489148b8e378e6168ddd9dbba18200f10f2788df69aa7100d20b341116102fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c22ab531-3377-5827-93d2-a13854b3308c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5492" :question/problem "![](images/15/1503285c91d0c320a3720c702b620bfe2156ed713d915b04c22bd35c06a05cd8.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3=\\big[ a, \\, b, \\, c \\big]^T$ such that the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Thus, the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ must correspond to $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain $x_1=x_2=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/7a/7aab21fae627c5fe36b144f4a1e12a2d5e57ef7c2c987fbee64f718c7cbb7404.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/c0/c080641a794b7a65f7893429d32920f39bfb4d87d9193197afd6b48dacc697a3.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{c}=\\dfrac{0}{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07180a2c-0c77-5f26-9a2f-445f5fc95d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269335} {:answer/id #uuid "0fab8a85-6645-511e-917e-cfb667d6120c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269409} {:answer/id #uuid "5993b1b8-7a24-54a3-a970-1281aa7648ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270064} {:answer/id #uuid "91813f30-028d-5290-b68b-e6c25df1898e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270535} {:answer/id #uuid "d737dd57-ceee-5d0d-bcad-1e441fa83cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d737dd57-ceee-5d0d-bcad-1e441fa83cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271162} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4772e05-c192-5a89-8d32-9aa2aa35ef84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f235e8a4-87f8-5bc9-a7bf-ca7b62641c4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140719" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2(0)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31dcee0a-b3fd-57c4-97a4-a11665baed4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269719} {:answer/id #uuid "84c9b455-f22d-5907-ad80-413eec64e049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270414} {:answer/id #uuid "85eb0455-8d8c-5fc3-86b6-d1a10c4afe7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270431} {:answer/id #uuid "b9539882-4d96-5e96-8872-29f5b35c6107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270905} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb05c8c-f619-5174-8242-5d23cb7c21f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85eb0455-8d8c-5fc3-86b6-d1a10c4afe7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270431} :answer-field/id #uuid "c58f795f-dc1c-59c4-9499-e84bc1457919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3c0726d-3efe-576b-8418-4bde00e5fffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140720" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{1}=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1423f464-beef-5e99-9409-b3d4ac5043ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321310} {:answer/id #uuid "7b755dec-3573-55e0-b187-1c70939adcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120} {:answer/id #uuid "806e3e30-0291-58e6-abb6-ecce94953100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322156} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce25d70-41a1-50f0-91bf-535055888ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322384} {:answer/id #uuid "b718991a-198e-56d5-a300-ebd932e0ab6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b755dec-3573-55e0-b187-1c70939adcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea0db33-c7cf-5915-89f1-c5178160661f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65ec0581-72cb-57ee-83e7-13061ed24107" :question/math-academy-id "q-36947" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -27 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027ec94f-19b6-5023-a751-ea66e6c774cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321172} {:answer/id #uuid "33c6f038-3292-5d96-8939-73910cb0ca3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321539} {:answer/id #uuid "5062acb5-1552-5a1b-a05e-498302f0bca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321777} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a70739-2d15-550b-8baa-0aa77a2ae01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322479} {:answer/id #uuid "e465570e-2679-52b8-900b-6763204ae5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a70739-2d15-550b-8baa-0aa77a2ae01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322479} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e8d110-3d3c-50c7-a33b-e2f79d4ff5f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b34e67f3-8e1a-5e70-994b-c21fe203d438" :question/math-academy-id "q-36951" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a29aa16-6c63-5a61-91a9-1f74b2ed3f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321225} {:answer/id #uuid "139de0b1-a7cc-5a6f-8d11-174a489bfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321305} {:answer/id #uuid "3a995098-985b-5a0d-8363-a15a9776bdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} {:answer/id #uuid "76e233f9-7d82-54a2-b6d4-56c363f79142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322074} {:answer/id #uuid "a544f028-4f1a-5cd3-8842-d28c764161a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a995098-985b-5a0d-8363-a15a9776bdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ac795a-9483-57f9-a1e0-931dea19d10f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f559f7f4-e02a-5090-9ef8-87c06ed535b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37399" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}]}
